    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">political party</span> is an organised group of people with shared political beliefs and ideologies who come together to contest elections and control government. Parties are essential to democratic governance.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Functions of Political Parties</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Contesting elections and seeking to form government</li><li>Political recruitment — identifying and producing leaders</li><li>Political socialisation — educating citizens on political issues</li><li>Aggregating and articulating interests of various groups</li><li>Forming government policy (when in power)</li><li>Providing political opposition and alternatives</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Types of Party Systems</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One-party system</td><td>Only one legal party; no competition</td><td>North Korea, China (CCP), historical Soviet Union</td></tr><tr><td>Two-party system</td><td>Two dominant parties alternate in power</td><td>USA (Democrats/Republicans), UK (Labour/Conservatives)</td></tr><tr><td>Multi-party system</td><td>Several parties compete; coalitions common</td><td>Nigeria, Germany, France, Israel</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Nigerian Political Parties</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Nigeria operates a multi-party system. Current major parties include: APC (All Progressives Congress), PDP (Peoples Democratic Party), LP (Labour Party), NNPP (New Nigeria Peoples Party). INEC registers and regulates political parties under the Electoral Act.</p><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Party Ideology</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Conservatism:</strong> tradition, limited government change, free market</li><li><strong>Liberalism:</strong> individual rights, social equality, progressive change</li><li><strong>Socialism/Social Democracy:</strong> social welfare, government intervention, equality</li><li><strong>Nationalism:</strong> promotion of national interests, sovereignty</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Nigeria party history:</strong> First Republic: NPC, NCNC, AG. Second Republic: NPN, UPN, NPP, GNPP, PRP. Military era: various bans and state-created parties. Fourth Republic: PDP dominated until 2015; APC formed 2013 and won 2015.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Political party = organised group contesting elections. Functions: recruitment, socialisation, government formation. One-party (China), two-party (USA/UK), multi-party (Nigeria). Nigeria: APC, PDP, LP, NNPP among others. INEC registers parties.</div>",
