    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Public opinion</span> refers to the collective views, attitudes, and beliefs of the population on political issues. In a democracy, public opinion is an important force that shapes government policy and holds leaders accountable.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Importance</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Public opinion is the sum of individual opinions on public matters</li><li>Governments in democracies are sensitive to public opinion — it influences their policies and survival</li><li>Public opinion sets limits on what government can do</li><li>It is the basis for electoral accountability</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Factors Shaping Public Opinion</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Mass media:</strong> newspapers, radio, TV, social media (most powerful shaper)</li><li><strong>Education:</strong> shapes political awareness and values</li><li><strong>Family:</strong> transmits political values (political socialisation)</li><li><strong>Peer groups:</strong> friends and associates influence views</li><li><strong>Religion:</strong> religious leaders and institutions shape views on moral and political issues</li><li><strong>Ethnic and regional identity:</strong> influences views in diverse societies like Nigeria</li><li><strong>Political parties:</strong> shape views through campaigns and manifestos</li><li><strong>Pressure groups:</strong> campaigns and advocacy shape public views</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Measurement of Public Opinion</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Opinion polls and surveys</li><li>Elections (most direct expression of public opinion)</li><li>Referendums and plebiscites</li><li>Public protests and demonstrations</li><li>Media analysis and letters to editors</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Propaganda and Public Opinion</h3><p class=\"learn-p\">Propaganda: systematic dissemination of selective information to shape public opinion in a particular direction. Unlike education (which presents balanced information for critical thinking), propaganda is one-sided and designed to manipulate. Authoritarian governments use propaganda to maintain public support.</p><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Public Opinion vs Public Interest:</strong> Public opinion = what the public actually thinks. Public interest = what would genuinely benefit the public. These may not always coincide.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Public opinion = collective views on political issues. Shaped by: media, family, education, religion, ethnicity. Measured by: polls, elections, referendums, protests. Propaganda = one-sided information to manipulate opinion.</div>",
