    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Citizenship</span> refers to the legal status of a person as a member of a political community (state), with defined rights and responsibilities. A good citizen is one who actively fulfils their duties while enjoying their rights.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Ways of Acquiring Nigerian Citizenship</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>By birth:</strong> born in Nigeria and at least one parent is Nigerian, OR born outside Nigeria with at least one Nigerian parent (jus sanguinis)</li><li><strong>By registration:</strong> foreign women who marry Nigerian men; persons of African descent and Negro descent</li><li><strong>By naturalisation:</strong> foreigners who have lived in Nigeria for 15 years, are of good character, intend to remain, and are acceptable to the community</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Rights of Nigerian Citizens</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Right to life (Section 33)</li><li>Right to dignity of the human person (Section 34)</li><li>Right to personal liberty (Section 35)</li><li>Right to fair hearing (Section 36)</li><li>Right to private and family life (Section 37)</li><li>Right to freedom of thought, conscience, and religion (Section 38)</li><li>Right to freedom of expression and the press (Section 39)</li><li>Right to peaceful assembly and association (Section 40)</li><li>Right to freedom of movement (Section 41)</li><li>Right to freedom from discrimination (Section 42)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Duties of Nigerian Citizens</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Obeying laws of the land</li><li>Payment of taxes</li><li>Voting in elections</li><li>Defending the country</li><li>Loyalty to the constitution</li><li>Protecting public property</li><li>Community participation</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Naturalisation period in Nigeria:</strong> 15 years of lawful residence required. Contrast with some countries: USA (5 years), UK (5 years), Canada (3 years).</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Citizenship acquired by birth, registration, or naturalisation. Fundamental rights: Sections 33-46 of 1999 Constitution. Duties: obey laws, pay taxes, vote, defend country. Denationalisation only in very limited circumstances.</div>",
