    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">International relations</span> is the study of interactions between and among states, international organisations, and non-state actors in the global arena. It deals with diplomacy, foreign policy, conflict, cooperation, and international law.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Key Concepts</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>State sovereignty:</strong> the supreme authority of a state over its territory and internal affairs — the foundation of international relations</li><li><strong>Diplomacy:</strong> the peaceful management of relations between states through negotiation and representation</li><li><strong>Foreign policy:</strong> the strategy a state uses to interact with other states and international actors</li><li><strong>Balance of power:</strong> distribution of power among states to prevent any one state from dominating others</li><li><strong>Nationalism:</strong> devotion to one's nation; can drive foreign policy and international conflict</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Instruments of Foreign Policy</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Diplomacy (negotiation, treaties, embassies)</li><li>Economic instruments (trade, aid, sanctions)</li><li>Military force (as a last resort)</li><li>Propaganda and public diplomacy</li><li>International organisations (multilateralism)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types of International Relations</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bilateral</td><td>Between two states</td><td>Nigeria-USA economic agreements</td></tr><tr><td>Multilateral</td><td>Among many states</td><td>AU, UN, ECOWAS agreements</td></tr><tr><td>Regional</td><td>Among states in a region</td><td>ECOWAS, AU, ASEAN</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Theoretical Perspectives</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Realism:</strong> states pursue national interest and power; international relations is anarchic</li><li><strong>Liberalism:</strong> cooperation, institutions, and democracy can overcome anarchy</li><li><strong>Constructivism:</strong> ideas, identities, and norms shape state behaviour</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>Diplomatic tools:</strong> Summits (head of state meetings), embassies (permanent representation abroad), consulates (services to citizens abroad), ambassador (highest diplomatic representative).</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> International relations = state interactions globally. State sovereignty = supreme authority. Diplomacy = peaceful management of relations. Foreign policy instruments: diplomacy, economic measures, military. Bilateral (2 states), multilateral (many states).</div>",
