    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">International organisations</span> are bodies formed by two or more states to pursue common goals through institutionalised cooperation. They play a crucial role in maintaining peace, promoting development, and regulating international relations.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. The United Nations (UN)</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Founded: 1945 (after World War II), San Francisco Charter</li><li>Members: 193 states</li><li>Headquarters: New York, USA</li><li>Main organs: General Assembly, Security Council (5 permanent members with veto: USA, UK, France, Russia, China), Secretariat (Secretary-General), International Court of Justice, ECOSOC, Trusteeship Council</li><li>Purposes: maintain international peace, develop friendly relations, promote cooperation on economic/social/cultural problems</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. The African Union (AU)</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Founded: 2002 (replaced OAU which was founded 1963)</li><li>Members: 55 African states</li><li>Headquarters: Addis Ababa, Ethiopia</li><li>Key organs: Assembly (heads of state), Executive Council, Commission, Pan-African Parliament, Peace and Security Council</li><li>Agenda 2063: Africa's development blueprint</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. ECOWAS (Economic Community of West African States)</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Founded: 1975 (Treaty of Lagos)</li><li>Members: 15 West African states</li><li>Headquarters: Abuja, Nigeria</li><li>Functions: regional economic integration, free movement of people/goods, peace and security (ECOMOG)</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Other Key Organisations</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>WTO: regulates international trade</li><li>IMF: financial assistance and macroeconomic stabilisation</li><li>World Bank: development financing for poor countries</li><li>NATO: North Atlantic military alliance</li><li>OPEC: oil-producing countries coordinating oil production</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>UN Security Council veto:</strong> USA, UK, France, Russia, China (P5) can each veto any UNSC resolution. This can paralyse action when P5 disagree.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> UN (1945, 193 members, NY). AU (2002, 55 African states, Addis Ababa). ECOWAS (1975, 15 states, Abuja). P5 veto in UNSC. ECOMOG = ECOWAS military wing used in Liberia/Sierra Leone.</div>",
