    "contentHTML": "<div class=\"learn-intro\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Human rights</span> are the basic rights and freedoms that belong to every person by virtue of being human — inherent, universal, indivisible, and inalienable. They protect individuals from abuses of power and ensure basic dignity for all.</div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Characteristics of Human Rights</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Universal:</strong> apply to all human beings everywhere, regardless of nationality, race, gender, religion</li><li><strong>Inalienable:</strong> cannot be permanently taken away or surrendered</li><li><strong>Indivisible:</strong> cannot prioritise one right over another; all are equally important</li><li><strong>Interdependent:</strong> the realisation of one right depends on the realisation of others</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Generations of Human Rights</h3><div class=\"learn-table-wrap\"><table class=\"learn-table\"><thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First generation</td><td>Civil and political rights (negative rights)</td><td>Right to life, fair trial, freedom of speech, voting rights, non-torture</td></tr><tr><td>Second generation</td><td>Economic, social, and cultural rights (positive rights)</td><td>Right to education, health, work, housing, social security</td></tr><tr><td>Third generation</td><td>Solidarity/collective rights</td><td>Right to development, clean environment, self-determination, peace</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Key International Human Rights Documents</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li><strong>Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR, 1948):</strong> the foundational document adopted by the UN General Assembly</li><li><strong>International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (ICCPR, 1966)</strong></li><li><strong>International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights (ICESCR, 1966)</strong></li><li><strong>African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights (Banjul Charter, 1981):</strong> African regional human rights instrument</li></ul><h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Human Rights in Nigeria</h3><ul class=\"learn-list\"><li>Chapter IV of 1999 Constitution: Fundamental Rights (Sections 33-46)</li><li>Human rights violations: police brutality (SARS), extrajudicial killings, torture, detention without trial</li><li>NHRC (National Human Rights Commission): government body for human rights education and complaints</li><li>Enforcement: courts (including ECOWAS Court), civil society organisations (Amnesty International, SERAP)</li></ul><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span><strong>UDHR (1948):</strong> Eleanor Roosevelt chaired the drafting committee. 30 articles covering all three generations (though mainly first generation). Not legally binding BUT forms basis of all subsequent binding human rights treaties.</div><div class=\"learn-tip-box\"><span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">🎯</span><strong>Summary:</strong> Human rights: universal, inalienable, indivisible. 1st gen (civil/political), 2nd gen (economic/social), 3rd gen (solidarity). UDHR 1948. African Charter 1981. Nigeria: Chapter IV rights. NHRC. SARS/EndSARS 2020.</div>",
